On my way home from the doctor, I go into the local pharmacy.
I pull a number from the machine and wait a little.
A friendly pharmacist calls number thirty-five.
I walk over to the counter and show my yellow health card.
She finds my prescription on the computer.
The pharmacist gives me a small box of allergy pills.
"You ought to take one pill every morning," she says.
I ask her if she has something for my throat.
"My throat still hurts," I explain.
She nods and suggests some sweet throat drops.
"They help when your throat hurts," she says.
"And you ought to drink a lot of water today as well."
I thank her and buy the pills and the drops.
It costs eighty-five kroner in total.
I am still tired and sore, but I am glad to have the medicine.
Now I just want to go home to my own sofa.
